This style guide demonstrates the typographic elements and styling available on this site. It serves as both a reference and a testing ground for visual consistency across different content types.

## Headings

Headings establish hierarchy and help readers navigate content. This site uses a clear progression from h2 through h4 for article content (h1 serves as the page title).

### Third level heading

Third level headings break major sections into logical subsections. Use them sparingly to avoid fragmenting the reading experience.

#### Fourth level heading

Fourth level headings work well for fine-grained organization within subsections. Beyond this level, consider restructuring the content instead of adding more heading levels.

## Paragraphs and text

Body text is the foundation of any reading experience. Good typography makes text disappear, allowing ideas to flow directly into the reader's mind without friction.

Short paragraphs work well for emphasis or transition. They give the eye a rest.

Longer paragraphs are essential for developing complex ideas. When an argument requires more than one sentence to unfold, or when a narrative needs room to breathe, the paragraph expands to accommodate. The key is matching paragraph length to content: dense technical explanations might warrant shorter paragraphs, while flowing prose can sustain longer ones. A comfortable reading measure of 60-75 characters per line helps maintain comprehension regardless of paragraph length.

## Lists

Lists organize information for quick scanning and reference.

### Unordered lists

Use unordered lists when the sequence of items does not matter:

- First item in the list
- Second item with more detail that wraps when the content runs long enough to show wrapping behavior
- Third item
- Fourth item with a sub-list:
  - Nested item one
  - Nested item two
  - Nested item three
- Fifth item returning to the main level

### Ordered lists

Use ordered lists when sequence or ranking matters:

1. Clone the repository from GitHub
2. Install dependencies with the package manager
3. Configure environment variables
4. Start the development server
5. Open the browser to localhost

## Blockquotes

Blockquotes highlight quotations or emphasize important passages:

> The goal is not to use interesting technology. The goal is to solve problems for users. Sometimes that means using the same tools everyone else uses.

Blockquotes can also span paragraphs:

> Choose boring technology. The databases that have been around for decades. The programming languages that your parents might recognize.
>
> PostgreSQL might not be exciting, but it will not wake you up at 3 AM with unexpected behavior.

## Inline formatting

You can style text in different ways for emphasis and clarity:

- **Bold text** draws attention to important terms or concepts
- *Italic text* provides subtle emphasis or indicates titles
- ***Bold and italic*** together for strong emphasis (use sparingly)
- `Inline code` highlights technical terms, file names, or short code snippets
- [Links](/about) connect to other pages or external resources
- [External links](https://example.com) point to resources outside this site

## Code blocks

Code blocks display longer code samples with syntax highlighting.

### JavaScript

```javascript
// A simple configuration example
const config = {
  database: {
    host: process.env.DB_HOST || "localhost",
    port: parseInt(process.env.DB_PORT, 10) || 5432,
    name: process.env.DB_NAME || "myapp",
  },
  cache: {
    ttl: 3600,
    maxSize: 1000,
  },
};

export default config;
```

### TypeScript

```typescript
interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  createdAt: Date;
}

function createUser(data: Omit<User, "id" | "createdAt">): User {
  return {
    id: crypto.randomUUID(),
    createdAt: new Date(),
    ...data,
  };
}
```

### Python

```python
from dataclasses import dataclass
from datetime import datetime
from typing import Optional


@dataclass
class Post:
    title: str
    content: str
    published_at: datetime
    updated_at: Optional[datetime] = None
    draft: bool = False

    def is_published(self) -> bool:
        return not self.draft and self.published_at <= datetime.now()
```

